          "text": "The scheduler cannot know how long a process will run. So it <em>predicts</em> the next burst from the previous ones using exponential averaging: the new prediction is a mix of the most recent actual burst and the old prediction. With α = 0.5 both count equally; with α = 0 the prediction never changes; with α = 1 only the last burst matters. This is the honest answer to 'how is SJF implemented in practice'."
        },
        {
          "title": "Starvation — and the cure",
          "text": "A process with a 50 ms burst may sit forever while 2 ms jobs keep arriving. This is called <strong>starvation</strong> or indefinite blocking. The standard cure is <strong>aging</strong>: slowly increase the priority of a process the longer it waits, so eventually it must be chosen. HRRN, the next topic, builds this cure directly into its formula."
