      "realLife": "At the medical shop, if the person ahead of you has a ten-item prescription and you only want one strip of Crocin, everyone finishes faster if the shopkeeper hands you the strip first. But if customers with one item keep arriving, the person with the long list may stand there all evening — that is starvation.",
      "worked": "<strong>Part A — SJF (non-preemptive)</strong><br>Same four processes as FCFS, all arriving at time 0:<br>P1 = 5, P2 = 3, P3 = 8, P4 = 4<br><br>Sort by burst: P2 (3), P4 (4), P1 (5), P3 (8).<br><br>Gantt chart:<br><code>| P2 | P4 | P1 | P3 |</code><br><code>0....3....7...12...20</code><br><br>Completion: P2 = 3, P4 = 7, P1 = 12, P3 = 20<br>Turnaround (arrival 0, so = completion): P2 = 3, P4 = 7, P1 = 12, P3 = 20<br>Waiting = TAT − Burst:<br>P2 = 3 − 3 = <strong>0</strong> · P4 = 7 − 4 = <strong>3</strong> · P1 = 12 − 5 = <strong>7</strong> · P3 = 20 − 8 = <strong>12</strong><br><br>Average Waiting Time = (0 + 3 + 7 + 12) / 4 = 22 / 4 = <strong>5.50 ms</strong><br>Average Turnaround Time = (3 + 7 + 12 + 20) / 4 = 42 / 4 = <strong>10.50 ms</strong><br><em>Check:</em> 5.50 + 5 = 10.50 ✓ &nbsp; (FCFS gave 7.25 and 12.25 — SJF is clearly better.)<br><br><strong>Part B — SRTN (preemptive)</strong><br>Now with different arrival times:<br><strong>Process&nbsp;&nbsp;Arrival&nbsp;&nbsp;Burst</strong><br>P1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;8<br>P2&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;4<br>P3&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;9<br>P4&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;5<br><br><em>t = 0:</em> only P1 is here. Run P1.<br><em>t = 1:</em> P2 arrives with burst 4. P1 has 7 left. 4 &lt; 7 → <strong>preempt P1</strong>, run P2.<br><em>t = 2:</em> P3 arrives with 9. P2 has 3 left. 3 is smallest → P2 continues.<br><em>t = 3:</em> P4 arrives with 5. P2 has 2 left. P2 continues.<br><em>t = 5:</em> P2 finishes. Remaining: P1 = 7, P3 = 9, P4 = 5. Smallest is P4 → run P4.<br><em>t = 10:</em> P4 finishes. Remaining: P1 = 7, P3 = 9. Run P1.<br><em>t = 17:</em> P1 finishes. Run P3 until 26.<br><br>Gantt chart:<br><code>| P1 | P2 | P4  | P1  | P3  |</code><br><code>0....1....5....10....17....26</code><br><br>Completion: P1 = 17, P2 = 5, P3 = 26, P4 = 10<br>Turnaround = Completion − Arrival:<br>P1 = 17 − 0 = 17 · P2 = 5 − 1 = 4 · P3 = 26 − 2 = 24 · P4 = 10 − 3 = 7<br>Waiting = TAT − Burst:<br>P1 = 17 − 8 = <strong>9</strong> · P2 = 4 − 4 = <strong>0</strong> · P3 = 24 − 9 = <strong>15</strong> · P4 = 7 − 5 = <strong>2</strong><br><br>Average Waiting Time = (9 + 0 + 15 + 2) / 4 = 26 / 4 = <strong>6.50 ms</strong><br>Average Turnaround Time = (17 + 4 + 24 + 7) / 4 = 52 / 4 = <strong>13.00 ms</strong><br><em>Check:</em> average burst = 26/4 = 6.5, and 6.50 + 6.50 = 13.00 ✓",
      "mistake": "In SRTN students compare the new arrival's burst with the running process's ORIGINAL burst. Wrong — always compare with the <strong>remaining</strong> time. At t = 1 above, P1's remaining time is 7, not 8.",
      "examAnswer": "Shortest Job First is a non-preemptive scheduling algorithm that allocates the processor to the ready process having the smallest next CPU burst, and it is provably optimal in the sense that it yields the minimum average waiting time for a given set of processes. Shortest Run Time Next, also called Shortest Remaining Time First, is its preemptive counterpart, in which the arrival of a process whose burst is shorter than the remaining time of the running process causes an immediate preemption. The principal limitations of both are that the length of the next CPU burst cannot be known in advance and must be estimated by exponential averaging, and that processes with long bursts may suffer indefinite starvation.",
      "remember": "SJF = shortest first, best average, but you must guess the future. SRTN = SJF that can interrupt.",
      "why": "SJF's optimality and its two problems (prediction and starvation) are a standard theory question, and the SRTN numerical is a standard 7-mark numerical.",
      "practice": "Processes P1(arrival 0, burst 7), P2(arrival 2, burst 4), P3(arrival 4, burst 1), P4(arrival 5, burst 4). Draw the SRTN Gantt chart and compute the average waiting time."
