    {
      "title": "Round Robin Scheduling",
      "teacher": "Imagine a teacher with twelve students and only one microphone. Give each student exactly one minute, then pass it on, round and round. Nobody is ignored and everybody gets to speak quickly. That, exactly, is Round Robin.",
      "explain": "<strong>Round Robin (RR)</strong> gives every process a fixed slice of CPU time called the <strong>time quantum</strong> (typically 10 to 100 ms). When the quantum expires, the process is preempted and sent to the <strong>back</strong> of the ready queue, and the next process runs. It is FCFS plus preemption, and it is the classic algorithm for time-sharing systems.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Preemptive; the ready queue is circular (FIFO with wrap-around).",
