      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "Choosing the time quantum",
          "text": "This is the whole design problem of RR. Too large and you lose interactivity — the system behaves like FCFS. Too small and the CPU spends most of its time switching instead of working. The quantum must be much larger than the context-switch time, which is why real systems use 10 to 100 ms against a switch cost of a few microseconds."
        },
        {
          "title": "What RR is really good at",
          "text": "RR is not trying to win on average waiting time — it will usually lose that to SJF. It is trying to make sure <em>nobody waits long for their first turn</em>. In our worked example below, RR gives an average response time of 4.5 ms against FCFS's 7.25 ms. That is the number that decides whether your machine feels responsive."
        },
        {
          "title": "Where a returning process goes",
          "text": "When a process uses up its quantum it joins the <strong>back</strong> of the queue. If another process arrives at exactly the same instant, the usual convention in textbook problems is that the <em>new arrival</em> joins the queue first and the preempted process goes behind it. State whichever convention you use in your answer — examiners accept either as long as you are consistent."
