      ],
      "realLife": "Your college computer lab has one printer and a 2-minute limit per student. Nobody can hog it with a 200-page document — after two minutes they go back to the end of the line. Everyone gets something printed quickly, though the big document takes many rounds to finish.",
      "worked": "<strong>Example</strong> — same four processes, all arriving at time 0, queue order P1, P2, P3, P4, <strong>time quantum = 3 ms</strong>.<br>P1 = 5, P2 = 3, P3 = 8, P4 = 4<br><br><strong>Step-by-step</strong> (remaining time shown in brackets):<br><em>0–3:</em> P1 runs 3 → P1 has 2 left, goes to back. Queue: P2, P3, P4, P1<br><em>3–6:</em> P2 runs 3 → P2 has 0 → <strong>P2 completes at 6</strong>. Queue: P3, P4, P1<br><em>6–9:</em> P3 runs 3 → P3 has 5 left, goes to back. Queue: P4, P1, P3<br><em>9–12:</em> P4 runs 3 → P4 has 1 left, goes to back. Queue: P1, P3, P4<br><em>12–14:</em> P1 runs its last 2 → <strong>P1 completes at 14</strong>. Queue: P3, P4<br><em>14–17:</em> P3 runs 3 → P3 has 2 left, goes to back. Queue: P4, P3<br><em>17–18:</em> P4 runs its last 1 → <strong>P4 completes at 18</strong>. Queue: P3<br><em>18–20:</em> P3 runs its last 2 → <strong>P3 completes at 20</strong><br><br><strong>Gantt chart</strong>:<br><code>| P1 | P2 | P3 | P4 | P1 | P3 | P4| P3 |</code><br><code>0....3....6....9...12...14...17..18...20</code><br><br><strong>Completion:</strong> P1 = 14, P2 = 6, P3 = 20, P4 = 18<br><strong>Turnaround</strong> (arrival 0): P1 = 14, P2 = 6, P3 = 20, P4 = 18<br><strong>Waiting = TAT − Burst:</strong><br>P1 = 14 − 5 = <strong>9</strong><br>P2 = 6 − 3 = <strong>3</strong><br>P3 = 20 − 8 = <strong>12</strong><br>P4 = 18 − 4 = <strong>14</strong><br><br>Average Waiting Time = (9 + 3 + 12 + 14) / 4 = 38 / 4 = <strong>9.50 ms</strong><br>Average Turnaround Time = (14 + 6 + 20 + 18) / 4 = 58 / 4 = <strong>14.50 ms</strong><br><em>Check:</em> 9.50 + 5 = 14.50 ✓<br><br><strong>But look at response time</strong> (first time each process ran):<br>P1 = 0, P2 = 3, P3 = 6, P4 = 9 → average = 18 / 4 = <strong>4.50 ms</strong><br>Under FCFS the response times were 0, 5, 8, 16 → average = <strong>7.25 ms</strong>.<br>So RR is worse on waiting time but much better on response time. That is the whole trade-off.",
      "mistake": "Two classic errors. (1) Putting a preempted process at the FRONT of the queue — it must go to the back. (2) Giving a process a full quantum when it only needs part of it: if 2 ms of burst remain and the quantum is 3 ms, it runs only 2 ms and then leaves the CPU immediately.",
      "examAnswer": "Round Robin is a preemptive scheduling algorithm designed for time-sharing systems, in which each process is allocated a fixed time quantum of the processor. If a process does not complete within its quantum it is preempted and placed at the tail of the ready queue, and the processor is given to the next process. Round Robin guarantees a bounded response time and is free from starvation, but it generally yields a higher average turnaround time than Shortest Job First. Its performance depends critically on the size of the time quantum: an excessively large quantum degenerates into First Come First Serve, whereas an excessively small quantum causes the context-switching overhead to dominate.",
      "remember": "Big quantum → becomes FCFS. Tiny quantum → drowns in context switches. Preempted process goes to the BACK.",
      "why": "Round Robin is the most commonly asked numerical in this unit, and the quantum trade-off is the most commonly asked theory follow-up.",
      "practice": "Processes P1 = 4, P2 = 5, P3 = 2, P4 = 1 all arrive at time 0 in that order. Draw the Round Robin Gantt chart for quantum = 2 and find the average waiting time."
