    {
      "title": "Highest Response Ratio Next (HRRN)",
      "teacher": "SJF has one ugly flaw — a big job can wait forever. So somebody asked a clever question: what if waiting itself slowly increased your priority? Wait long enough and you must get your turn. That simple idea is HRRN.",
      "explain": "<strong>HRRN</strong> is a non-preemptive algorithm that, whenever the CPU becomes free, computes a <strong>response ratio</strong> for every waiting process and picks the highest one. The formula is <strong>Response Ratio = (W + S) / S</strong>, where W is how long the process has already waited and S is its service (burst) time. Because W keeps growing, no process can starve.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "Formula: <strong>Response Ratio = (Waiting time + Service time) / Service time</strong>.",
        "An equivalent form is <strong>1 + (W / S)</strong> — useful for quick mental checks.",
        "Non-preemptive: ratios are computed only when the CPU becomes free.",
        "The minimum possible ratio is <strong>1</strong> (a process that has not waited at all).",
        "Short jobs get a high ratio quickly (small S), so HRRN still favours short jobs like SJF.",
        "Long jobs get a rising ratio as W grows, so they <strong>cannot starve</strong> — aging is built into the formula.",
        "It still needs the service time S to be known or estimated, exactly like SJF.",
        "Designed as a compromise between FCFS (fair) and SJF (efficient)."
