      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "Reading the formula correctly",
          "text": "In (W + S) / S, the numerator W + S is what the turnaround time <em>would be</em> if the process started right now, and the denominator S is the work it actually needs. So the ratio is really 'how much total time will this cost me for each unit of useful work'. A high ratio means the process has been punished a lot relative to its size — so serve it."
        },
        {
          "title": "How it kills starvation",
          "text": "Take a long job with S = 100. At first W = 0 and the ratio is only 1.0, so short jobs beat it. But after waiting 400 units, its ratio becomes (400 + 100) / 100 = 5.0, which beats a fresh 2-unit job whose ratio is only 1.0. Waiting, by itself, eventually wins. This is exactly the aging idea written as a formula."
        },
        {
          "title": "The order of work in a numerical",
          "text": "Recompute the ratios at every point where the CPU becomes free — <em>only</em> for processes that have already arrived by that moment. Do not include a process that has not arrived yet, and do not recompute in the middle of a running process, because HRRN is non-preemptive. Write the ratio values in your answer sheet; examiners give marks for the working, not just the final Gantt chart."
