      "realLife": "A good doctor's clinic: normally the quickest cases go first, but the receptionist keeps noting how long each patient has been sitting. Somebody who has waited three hours gets called even though his case is long — because waiting itself has earned him priority.",
      "worked": "<strong>Example</strong><br><strong>Process&nbsp;&nbsp;Arrival&nbsp;&nbsp;Burst (S)</strong><br>P1&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;0&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3<br>P2&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;6<br>P3&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;4<br>P4&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;4&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;5<br><br><strong>Decision at t = 0</strong> — only P1 has arrived, so run P1 from 0 to 3.<br><br><strong>Decision at t = 3</strong> — arrived and waiting: P2 (came at 2), P3 (came at 3).<br>P2: W = 3 − 2 = 1, S = 6 → RR = (1 + 6) / 6 = 7/6 = <strong>1.167</strong><br>P3: W = 3 − 3 = 0, S = 4 → RR = (0 + 4) / 4 = <strong>1.000</strong><br>Highest is P2 → run P2 from 3 to 9.<br><br><strong>Decision at t = 9</strong> — waiting: P3 (came at 3), P4 (came at 4).<br>P3: W = 9 − 3 = 6, S = 4 → RR = (6 + 4) / 4 = 10/4 = <strong>2.50</strong><br>P4: W = 9 − 4 = 5, S = 5 → RR = (5 + 5) / 5 = 10/5 = <strong>2.00</strong><br>Highest is P3 → run P3 from 9 to 13.<br><br><strong>Decision at t = 13</strong> — only P4 left → run P4 from 13 to 18.<br><br><strong>Gantt chart</strong>:<br><code>| P1 | P2  | P3 | P4 |</code><br><code>0....3.....9...13...18</code><br><br><strong>Completion:</strong> P1 = 3, P2 = 9, P3 = 13, P4 = 18<br><strong>Turnaround = Completion − Arrival:</strong><br>P1 = 3 − 0 = 3 · P2 = 9 − 2 = 7 · P3 = 13 − 3 = 10 · P4 = 18 − 4 = 14<br><strong>Waiting = TAT − Burst:</strong><br>P1 = 3 − 3 = <strong>0</strong> · P2 = 7 − 6 = <strong>1</strong> · P3 = 10 − 4 = <strong>6</strong> · P4 = 14 − 5 = <strong>9</strong><br><br>Average Waiting Time = (0 + 1 + 6 + 9) / 4 = 16 / 4 = <strong>4.00 units</strong><br>Average Turnaround Time = (3 + 7 + 10 + 14) / 4 = 34 / 4 = <strong>8.50 units</strong><br><em>Check:</em> average burst = (3+6+4+5)/4 = 4.5, and 4.00 + 4.50 = 8.50 ✓",
      "mistake": "Do not write the formula upside down as S / (W + S) — the ratio must GROW as waiting grows. And do not recompute ratios in the middle of a running process: HRRN is non-preemptive, so ratios are only compared when the CPU actually becomes free.",
      "examAnswer": "Highest Response Ratio Next is a non-preemptive scheduling algorithm in which, whenever the processor becomes available, the response ratio of every waiting process is computed as (W + S) divided by S, where W denotes the time the process has already spent waiting and S denotes its estimated service time, and the process with the highest ratio is selected. Because short processes possess a small denominator they obtain a high ratio quickly, while long processes accumulate an increasing numerator as they wait. HRRN therefore retains much of the efficiency of Shortest Job First while eliminating starvation, and is regarded as a compromise between First Come First Serve and Shortest Job First.",
      "remember": "RR = (Waiting + Service) / Service. Wait longer, rank higher. Nobody starves.",
      "why": "HRRN is the standard answer to 'how can starvation in SJF be avoided?' — a question that appears in both theory and numerical form.",
      "practice": "P1 (arrival 0, burst 4), P2 (arrival 1, burst 3), P3 (arrival 2, burst 6). Apply HRRN, showing every response ratio you compute, and find the average waiting time."
