      "examAnswer": "A multilevel queue scheduling algorithm partitions the ready queue into several separate queues of differing priority, each of which may employ its own scheduling algorithm, and a process is permanently assigned to one queue. A multilevel feedback queue extends this scheme by permitting processes to migrate between queues according to their observed behaviour: a process that consumes its entire time quantum is moved to a lower-priority queue with a larger quantum, whereas a process that waits excessively long may be promoted, a technique known as aging, which prevents starvation. This enables the operating system to distinguish interactive processes from CPU-bound processes automatically, and it is therefore the most general and most widely used scheduling scheme, although it requires the careful tuning of several parameters.",
